    summary: "Principles of cancer chemotherapy and the cell cycle; the major classes of cytotoxic drugs; common toxicities and their management; safe handling of cytotoxics; targeted therapy and immunosuppressants; and the nurse's role in supportive care and safety.",
    youtubeId: "", tutor: "",
    notes: [
      { h: "Principles of cancer chemotherapy", p: "Cancer chemotherapy uses cytotoxic drugs to kill rapidly dividing malignant cells. Because the drugs act on all rapidly dividing cells, they also damage normal fast-growing tissues — bone marrow, gut lining, hair follicles and reproductive cells — which explains most side effects. Drugs are often combined (different mechanisms, non-overlapping toxicities) and given in cycles to allow normal tissue to recover between courses." },
      { h: "The cell cycle and drug action", p: "Cells progress through G1 (growth), S (DNA synthesis), G2 (preparation) and M (mitosis) phases, or rest in G0. Cell-cycle-specific drugs act at a particular phase (e.g. antimetabolites in S phase, vinca alkaloids in M phase); cell-cycle-non-specific drugs (e.g. alkylating agents) act at any phase. This underlies scheduling and combination design.", figure: { caption: "The cell cycle and where cytotoxic drug classes act.", svg: "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cell cycle and drug action phases.\"><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"100\" r=\"70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#15803D\" stroke-width=\"10\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#0F4C3A\">M (mitosis)</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#0F4C3A\">G1</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#0F4C3A\">S (DNA)</text><text x=\"112\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#0F4C3A\">G2</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#15803D\">vinca: M</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"9\" fill=\"#15803D\">antimetab: S</text></svg>" } },
      { h: "Classes of cytotoxic drugs", list: ["Alkylating agents (cyclophosphamide, cisplatin) — cross-link DNA; cell-cycle-non-specific.", "Antimetabolites (methotrexate, 5-fluorouracil, cytarabine) — mimic normal metabolites to block DNA synthesis (S phase).", "Antitumour antibiotics (doxorubicin, bleomycin) — intercalate DNA; doxorubicin is cardiotoxic, bleomycin causes pulmonary fibrosis.", "Plant alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine, paclitaxel) — disrupt the mitotic spindle (M phase); vincristine causes neuropathy.", "Hormonal agents (tamoxifen, anti-androgens) — for hormone-sensitive cancers.", "Miscellaneous — platinum compounds, topoisomerase inhibitors."] },
      { h: "Common toxicities", list: ["Bone-marrow suppression (myelosuppression) — anaemia, neutropenia (infection risk), thrombocytopenia (bleeding); the dose-limiting toxicity for many drugs.", "Nausea and vomiting — often severe; managed with 5-HT3 antagonists, corticosteroids and other anti-emetics.", "Alopecia (hair loss) — usually reversible.", "Mucositis and diarrhoea — from gut epithelial damage.", "Extravasation — vesicant drugs leaking from the vein cause severe tissue necrosis.", "Hyperuricaemia (tumour lysis syndrome), infertility, and specific organ toxicities."] },
      { h: "Managing key toxicities", list: ["Neutropenia — monitor blood counts; neutropenic sepsis is a medical emergency (fever with low neutrophils) needing urgent antibiotics; use protective isolation and hand hygiene.", "Nausea/vomiting — pre-emptive anti-emetics before and after chemotherapy.", "Methotrexate toxicity — folinic acid (leucovorin) rescue.", "Doxorubicin — monitor cardiac function; know the lifetime cumulative dose limit.", "Uric acid — hydration and allopurinol/rasburicase to prevent tumour lysis syndrome."] },
      { h: "Safe handling of cytotoxic drugs", p: "Cytotoxics are hazardous to staff (mutagenic, teratogenic, carcinogenic). They must be prepared in a biological safety cabinet by trained staff wearing protective equipment; spills are managed with a spill kit; and waste and body fluids are disposed of as cytotoxic waste. Pregnant staff should avoid handling them." },
      { h: "Targeted therapy and immunosuppressants", list: ["Targeted agents — monoclonal antibodies (e.g. trastuzumab, rituximab) and tyrosine-kinase inhibitors (e.g. imatinib) act on specific molecular targets with a different toxicity profile.", "Immunosuppressants (azathioprine, ciclosporin, mycophenolate, tacrolimus) — used in transplantation and autoimmune disease; main risks are infection and malignancy from reduced immunity.", "Corticosteroids also have immunosuppressive and antineoplastic roles (e.g. in lymphomas)."] },
      { h: "Normal values / key figures", list: ["Neutropenic sepsis — fever in a neutropenic patient is a medical emergency.", "Folinic acid (leucovorin) — rescue for methotrexate toxicity.", "Doxorubicin — cardiotoxic with a lifetime cumulative dose limit.", "Cytotoxics are prepared in a biological safety cabinet with protective equipment."] },
      { h: "Combination chemotherapy and dosing", p: "Drugs are combined so that agents with different mechanisms and non-overlapping toxicities attack the tumour from several directions while limiting harm to any one normal tissue, and to reduce the emergence of resistant clones. Doses are frequently calculated by body surface area for greater accuracy, and treatment is given in cycles timed to allow bone marrow and gut to recover before the next course." },
      { h: "Psychological and supportive care", p: "Beyond drug toxicity, chemotherapy has a heavy psychological burden — fear, altered body image from hair loss, fatigue and uncertainty. Supportive care includes clear information, anticipatory management of nausea and mucositis, nutritional support, and attention to fertility and sexual health. The nurse’s therapeutic relationship is central to helping the patient complete treatment." },
      { h: "Clinical relevance", p: "Applying knowledge of the cell cycle, toxicity patterns and safe handling lets the nurse anticipate the nadir of blood counts and act on neutropenic fever as an emergency, prevent rather than react to nausea, protect against extravasation, and safeguard staff from hazardous exposure. This turns cytotoxic pharmacology into safe, humane cancer nursing." },
      { h: "Nursing responsibilities", list: ["Monitor full blood count; recognise and urgently escalate neutropenic sepsis.", "Give anti-emetics pre-emptively; assess and manage mucositis with mouth care.", "Ensure secure IV access and monitor for extravasation of vesicants; know the local extravasation protocol.", "Follow safe-handling, spill and cytotoxic-waste procedures strictly.", "Provide psychological support and clear information about side effects and their management.", "Educate on infection precautions, adequate hydration and reporting fever, bleeding or bruising."] }
    ],
